// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 10): space.
ADDART({id:"bepicolombo",t:"BepiColombo",c:"Space Exploration",i:"☿️",w:"BepiColombo",r:["mercury","spaceagencies","ionengines","venus","japancountry"],
p:[["A joint mission to Mercury","BepiColombo is a European–Japanese mission to the innermost planet."],["A long journey","It took eight years and nine planetary flybys to reach Mercury."],["Arriving in November 2026","It was due to enter orbit around Mercury on 21 November 2026."]],
l:"BepiColombo is a joint mission of the European Space Agency (ESA) and the Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency (JAXA) to study Mercury, the smallest planet and the closest to the Sun. Named after the Italian scientist Giuseppe ‘Bepi’ Colombo, who worked out how the Mariner 10 spacecraft could fly past Mercury several times, it was launched on an Ariane 5 rocket on 20 October 2018. The spacecraft consists of two orbiters, ESA’s Mercury Planetary Orbiter and JAXA’s Mercury Magnetospheric Orbiter, named Mio, carried by a Mercury Transfer Module with electric thrusters. Reaching Mercury is surprisingly hard, because a spacecraft falling toward the Sun speeds up and must lose a lot of energy to be captured; BepiColombo used one flyby of Earth, two of Venus and six of Mercury to slow down. A thruster problem in 2024 delayed its arrival. The transfer module separated in September 2026, and on 21 November 2026 the still-joined spacecraft entered orbit around Mercury. The two orbiters are due to separate on 9–10 December 2026 and to start science operations in April 2027. They will study Mercury’s surface, interior, magnetic field and thin atmosphere and look for ice at its poles.",
s:[["Why Mercury is hard to reach","Because of the Sun’s strong gravity, reaching Mercury requires more change in speed than reaching Pluto. Missions must use gravity assists and engines to brake. Only two spacecraft had visited before: NASA’s Mariner 10 in 1974–1975 and MESSENGER, which orbited from 2011 to 2015."],["Two orbiters","ESA’s orbiter will map Mercury’s surface and study its composition and interior from a low polar orbit. Japan’s Mio will study Mercury’s magnetic field and how it interacts with the solar wind from a more stretched orbit."],["Surviving the heat","At Mercury, sunlight is up to ten times stronger than at Earth, and the planet’s surface reaches about 430°C. BepiColombo uses special insulation, radiators and heat-resistant materials to protect its instruments."],["Puzzles","Mercury has a surprisingly large iron core, a magnetic field, and dark material on its surface. MESSENGER found water ice in permanently shadowed polar craters and strange hollows. BepiColombo aims to explain how Mercury formed and why it is so dense."]],
k:[["Agencies","ESA and JAXA"],["Launch","20 October 2018"],["Flybys","1 Earth, 2 Venus, 6 Mercury"],["Mercury orbit insertion","21 November 2026"],["Science operations","From 2027"]],
d:[["2018","BepiColombo launches"],["2020","Earth flyby"],["2020–2021","Two Venus flybys"],["2021–2025","Six Mercury flybys"],["2024","Thruster problem delays arrival"],["2026","Enters orbit around Mercury (21 November)"]],
s2:[["Bepi Colombo","Giuseppe Colombo (1920–1984) was a mathematician and engineer at the University of Padua. He realized that Mariner 10’s orbit could be arranged to return to Mercury repeatedly and also proposed the idea of tethered satellites."],["Selfies at Mercury","During its flybys, BepiColombo’s small monitoring cameras photographed Mercury’s cratered surface with parts of the spacecraft in view, giving the public early glimpses of the planet."]],
sp:["Mercury’s day","Mercury rotates very slowly: one solar day, from sunrise to sunrise, lasts about 176 Earth days, twice as long as its year of 88 days."],
g:[["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"],["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change speed"],["Magnetosphere","The region controlled by a planet’s magnetic field"],["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles from the Sun"]],
f:["BepiColombo used nine planetary flybys.","Reaching Mercury takes more energy than reaching Pluto.","It entered orbit around Mercury on 21 November 2026."],
fun:"BepiColombo’s journey to Mercury took eight years, longer than the Cassini spacecraft took to reach Saturn."});

ADDART({id:"venusmissions",t:"Missions to Venus",c:"Space Exploration",i:"♀️",w:"Exploration of Venus",r:["venus","climchange","spaceagencies","parkerprobe","astrobio"],
p:[["Earth’s hellish twin","Venus is similar in size to Earth but has a crushing, scorching atmosphere."],["Soviet landers","The Soviet Venera probes were the first to land on another planet."],["New missions coming","NASA and ESA plan new Venus missions in the 2030s."]],
l:"Venus, the second planet from the Sun and the nearest planet to Earth, has been visited by more than 40 spacecraft. It was the first planet explored by spacecraft: NASA’s Mariner 2 flew past on 14 December 1962, confirming its extreme heat. The Soviet Union’s Venera program achieved remarkable firsts despite surface temperatures of about 465°C and pressure 90 times Earth’s: Venera 7 made the first soft landing on another planet on 15 December 1970, and Venera 9 sent the first images from the surface of another planet in 1975. Venera 13 sent color pictures in 1982 and survived for 127 minutes. NASA’s Magellan mapped almost the entire surface with radar from 1990 to 1994, revealing volcanoes and lava plains, while ESA’s Venus Express (2006–2014) and Japan’s Akatsuki (2015–2024) studied its atmosphere. In 2020, scientists reported possible phosphine gas in Venus’s clouds, a potential sign of life, though later studies questioned it. NASA’s DAVINCI probe is targeted for launch around 2030 to plunge through the atmosphere, while NASA’s VERITAS and ESA’s EnVision orbiters face budget uncertainty.",
s:[["Surviving the surface","Venera landers were built like pressure vessels and pre-cooled before entry. Even so, they survived only from about 23 minutes to two hours on the surface before heat and pressure destroyed them. Their images showed a flat, rocky landscape under an orange sky."],["Why Venus matters","Venus probably once had liquid water but suffered a runaway greenhouse effect, trapping heat under thick carbon dioxide clouds of sulfuric acid. Studying Venus helps scientists understand how planets evolve and the extremes of climate change."],["Volcanoes","Magellan revealed more than 1,600 major volcanoes. In 2023, scientists re-examining Magellan images found a volcanic vent that had changed shape between 1991 images, evidence that Venus is still volcanically active."],["The coming missions","DAVINCI will drop a probe through the atmosphere, measuring its chemistry and photographing the mountainous Alpha Regio region. VERITAS would map the surface in high resolution, and ESA’s EnVision would study the surface and interior. Budget pressures at NASA have slowed VERITAS and NASA’s role in EnVision."]],
k:[["First flyby","Mariner 2, 14 December 1962"],["First landing","Venera 7, 15 December 1970"],["Radar mapping","Magellan, 1990–1994"],["Surface temperature","About 465°C"],["Next mission","DAVINCI (targeted around 2030)"]],
d:[["1962","Mariner 2 flies past Venus"],["1970","Venera 7 lands on Venus"],["1975","Venera 9 sends the first surface images"],["1982","Venera 13 sends color images"],["1990–1994","Magellan maps Venus by radar"],["2020","Possible phosphine detection announced"],["2023","Evidence of active volcanism"]],
s2:[["Balloons on Venus","In 1985, the Soviet Vega missions released two balloons into Venus’s atmosphere, which floated for about two days at about 54 kilometers altitude, the first balloons flown on another planet."],["Phosphine debate","The 2020 phosphine report caused excitement because on Earth phosphine is linked to life. Reanalysis suggested the signal was weaker or could be sulfur dioxide, and the debate continues."]],
sp:["A day longer than a year","Venus rotates backward and extremely slowly: one rotation takes 243 Earth days, longer than its year of 225 days."],
g:[["Runaway greenhouse effect","Uncontrolled heating from trapped heat"],["Lander","A spacecraft that lands on a surface"],["Sulfuric acid","A highly corrosive acid"],["Radar mapping","Making maps with radio echoes"]],
f:["Venera 7 was the first craft to land on another planet.","Venus’s surface is about 465°C.","Venus is still volcanically active."],
fun:"Venus is so hot that lead would melt on its surface, yet about 50 kilometers up in its clouds, temperatures and pressures are similar to Earth’s."});

ADDART({id:"pioneerprobes",t:"Pioneer 10 and 11",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🛸",w:"Pioneer 10",r:["jupiter","saturn","voyager","fermiparadox","asteroidbelt"],
p:[["First to Jupiter","Pioneer 10 was the first spacecraft to cross the asteroid belt and fly past Jupiter."],["First to Saturn","Pioneer 11 made the first flyby of Saturn in 1979."],["A message to aliens","Both carry plaques showing humans and Earth’s location."]],
l:"Pioneer 10 and Pioneer 11 were NASA space probes that made the first journeys to the outer solar system. Pioneer 10, launched on 2 March 1972, became the first spacecraft to pass through the asteroid belt, showing it was safe to cross, and the first to fly past Jupiter, on 3 December 1973, returning close-up images and measuring the planet’s intense radiation. It then headed out of the solar system. Pioneer 11, launched on 5 April 1973, flew past Jupiter in December 1974 and used Jupiter’s gravity to reach Saturn, making the first flyby of Saturn on 1 September 1979 and discovering a new ring. Both probes were powered by radioisotope generators. Each carries a gold-anodized aluminum plaque, designed by Carl Sagan and Frank Drake with artwork by Linda Salzman Sagan, showing a man and a woman, the spacecraft and a map locating the Sun among pulsars. Contact with Pioneer 11 ended in 1995, and Pioneer 10’s last faint signal was received on 23 January 2003, when it was about 12 billion kilometers from Earth. Their discoveries paved the way for the Voyager missions.",
s:[["Crossing the asteroid belt","Before Pioneer 10, some scientists feared that dust and rocks in the asteroid belt would destroy spacecraft. Pioneer 10 crossed it in seven months without serious damage, proving it could be navigated."],["Jupiter’s radiation","Pioneer 10 found that Jupiter’s radiation belts were far more intense than expected, nearly damaging its electronics. This knowledge helped engineers protect later spacecraft such as Voyager and Galileo."],["The plaque","The plaque shows the hydrogen atom as a unit of measurement, the human figures in front of an outline of the spacecraft for scale, and the solar system with a line showing Pioneer’s path. The nude figures caused some public controversy in 1972."],["The Pioneer anomaly","Tracking showed that both Pioneers were slowing slightly more than expected, a puzzle called the Pioneer anomaly. In 2012, scientists concluded that it was caused by heat radiating unevenly from the spacecraft, not new physics."]],
k:[["Pioneer 10 launch","2 March 1972"],["First Jupiter flyby","3 December 1973"],["Pioneer 11 launch","5 April 1973"],["First Saturn flyby","1 September 1979"],["Last Pioneer 10 signal","23 January 2003"]],
d:[["1972","Pioneer 10 launches"],["1973","Pioneer 10 flies past Jupiter; Pioneer 11 launches"],["1974","Pioneer 11 flies past Jupiter"],["1979","Pioneer 11 makes the first Saturn flyby"],["1995","Contact with Pioneer 11 ends"],["2003","Last signal from Pioneer 10"],["2012","Pioneer anomaly explained"]],
s2:[["Where are they going?","Pioneer 10 is heading toward the red giant star Aldebaran in the constellation Taurus, which it would pass in about 2 million years. Pioneer 11 is heading toward the constellation Aquila."],["Voyager overtakes","Pioneer 10 was the most distant human-made object until Voyager 1, launched later but traveling faster, overtook it in 1998."]],
sp:["Linda Salzman Sagan","The artist who drew the human figures on the Pioneer plaque, Linda Salzman Sagan, later helped create the Voyager Golden Record with Carl Sagan, Frank Drake and others."],
g:[["Probe","An uncrewed spacecraft sent to explore"],["Radioisotope generator","A power source using heat from radioactive decay"],["Flyby","Passing close to an object"],["Pulsar","A rapidly spinning neutron star"]],
f:["Pioneer 10 was the first to fly past Jupiter.","Pioneer 11 made the first Saturn flyby.","Both carry plaques for aliens."],
fun:"The Pioneer plaque uses the pulses of 14 pulsars like a cosmic map to show where the Sun is in the galaxy."});

ADDART({id:"vikinglanders",t:"The Viking Mars Landers",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔴",w:"Viking program",r:["mars","marsrovers","astrobio","spaceagencies","conspiracy"],
p:[["First US Mars landings","Viking 1 and 2 landed on Mars in 1976."],["Searching for life","They carried experiments to look for microbes in Martian soil."],["Long-lasting","Viking 1’s lander worked for more than six years."]],
l:"The Viking program consisted of two NASA missions to Mars, Viking 1 and Viking 2, each made up of an orbiter and a lander. Launched in 1975, Viking 1’s lander touched down on the plain of Chryse Planitia on 20 July 1976, seven years to the day after Apollo 11’s Moon landing, becoming the first spacecraft to operate successfully on the surface of Mars for an extended period. Viking 2 landed on Utopia Planitia on 3 September 1976. The landers sent back the first close-up color pictures of the Martian surface, showing a rusty, rock-strewn desert under a pinkish sky, measured weather and seasons, and detected marsquakes. Most famously, they carried three biology experiments to search for microbial life in the soil. One, the Labeled Release experiment, gave results that seemed positive, but others found no organic molecules, and most scientists concluded that chemistry, not life, explained the results, though debate continues. The orbiters mapped Mars and photographed the ‘Face on Mars’. Viking 1’s lander worked until November 1982.",
s:[["Landing on Mars","Each lander was released from its orbiter, slowed by a heat shield, a parachute and finally retrorockets. Engineers sterilized the landers by baking them to avoid contaminating Mars with Earth microbes."],["The life experiments","Viking’s experiments added nutrients to soil samples and looked for gases that microbes might produce. The Labeled Release experiment detected gases, but the absence of organic molecules suggested reactive chemicals in the soil. In 2008, the Phoenix lander found perchlorates, which may explain the results."],["The ‘Face on Mars’","A 1976 Viking orbiter image showed a hill that looked like a human face because of lighting. Some people claimed it was an alien monument, but higher-resolution images in 1998 and 2001 showed an ordinary eroded mesa."],["Legacy","Viking’s weather station recorded Martian seasons for years, and its maps guided later missions. Its discoveries about Mars’s cold, dry, oxidizing surface shaped how scientists search for life there today."]],
k:[["Viking 1 landing","20 July 1976"],["Viking 2 landing","3 September 1976"],["Life experiments","Three biology tests"],["Lander lifetime","Viking 1: until November 1982"],["Famous image","The ‘Face on Mars’"]],
d:[["1975","Viking 1 and 2 launch"],["1976","Viking 1 lands (20 July)"],["1976","Viking 2 lands (3 September)"],["1980","Viking 2 lander stops working"],["1982","Viking 1 lander stops working"],["2001","Mars Global Surveyor images debunk the ‘Face’"]],
s2:[["Mars’s first color picture","When Viking 1 sent its first color image, engineers initially adjusted the colors to show a blue sky like Earth’s, before correcting them to the true butterscotch-pink sky."],["Gilbert Levin","Gilbert Levin, who designed the Labeled Release experiment, argued for the rest of his life that it had detected life. Most scientists disagreed, but his claim kept the question alive."]],
sp:["The Thomas Mutch Memorial Station","NASA renamed Viking 1’s landing site the Thomas Mutch Memorial Station after the leader of its imaging team, who died in a climbing accident in 1980."],
g:[["Lander","A spacecraft that touches down on a surface"],["Organic molecule","A carbon-based molecule"],["Perchlorate","A reactive salt found in Martian soil"],["Mesa","A flat-topped hill"]],
f:["Viking 1 landed on Mars on 20 July 1976.","Viking searched for life in Martian soil.","The ‘Face on Mars’ is an eroded hill."],
fun:"Viking’s lander cameras were so slow that each image was scanned line by line over several minutes."});

ADDART({id:"changeprogram",t:"China’s Chang’e Moon Missions",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌕",w:"Chinese Lunar Exploration Program",r:["moon","moonbases","tianwen","spaceagencies","chinacountry"],
p:[["Named after a Moon goddess","Chang’e is the Moon goddess in Chinese mythology."],["First far-side landing","Chang’e 4 made the first landing on the Moon’s far side in 2019."],["Far-side samples","Chang’e 6 brought back the first samples from the far side in 2024."]],
l:"The Chang’e program is China’s robotic lunar exploration program, named after Chang’e, the goddess of the Moon in Chinese mythology. Its missions have followed a step-by-step plan to orbit, land and return samples. Chang’e 1 (2007) and Chang’e 2 (2010) orbited and mapped the Moon. On 14 December 2013, Chang’e 3 made the first soft landing on the Moon since 1976, deploying the Yutu, or ‘Jade Rabbit’, rover. On 3 January 2019, Chang’e 4 made the first ever landing on the far side of the Moon, in the Von Kármán crater, communicating through a relay satellite called Queqiao; its Yutu-2 rover became the longest-operating lunar rover. Chang’e 5 returned about 1.7 kilograms of lunar samples in December 2020, the first since 1976, revealing volcanic activity more recent than expected. Chang’e 6 landed on the far side in June 2024 and returned about 1.9 kilograms of samples, the first from the far side. Chang’e 7, designed to search for water ice near the lunar south pole with an orbiter, lander, rover and hopper, was planned for launch in August 2026 but was postponed by bad weather and is now expected in 2027, and Chang’e 8 will test technologies for a future lunar research station.",
s:[["The far side","The far side of the Moon always faces away from Earth, so a lander there cannot communicate directly. China launched the Queqiao relay satellite to a point beyond the Moon in 2018. Chang’e 4 also grew cotton seeds in a small biosphere, the first plants to sprout on the Moon, though they soon died."],["Yutu-2","The Yutu-2 rover explored the far side for years, traveling more than 1.5 kilometers and studying the soil and rocks. Its radar revealed layers beneath the surface."],["Samples","Chang’e 5’s samples, from Oceanus Procellarum, showed lava flowed there about 2 billion years ago, younger than Apollo samples. Chang’e 6’s far-side samples, from the giant South Pole–Aitken basin, help explain why the near and far sides differ."],["International partners","Chang’e missions have carried instruments from countries including Sweden, Germany, France, Italy and Pakistan. China plans an International Lunar Research Station near the south pole with Russia and other partners, and crewed landings before 2030."]],
k:[["Named after","Chang’e, the Moon goddess"],["First far-side landing","Chang’e 4, 3 January 2019"],["Chang’e 5 samples","About 1.7 kg (2020)"],["Chang’e 6 samples","About 1.9 kg from the far side (2024)"],["Next mission","Chang’e 7 (south pole)"]],
d:[["2007","Chang’e 1 orbits the Moon"],["2013","Chang’e 3 lands with the Yutu rover"],["2019","Chang’e 4 lands on the far side"],["2020","Chang’e 5 returns samples"],["2024","Chang’e 6 returns far-side samples"]],
s2:[["The legend of Chang’e","In the legend, Chang’e drinks an elixir of immortality and floats to the Moon, where she lives with a jade rabbit. She is celebrated during the Mid-Autumn Festival with mooncakes."],["Apollo astronauts and the rabbit","During Apollo 11, mission control in Houston jokingly told the astronauts to watch out for ‘a lovely girl with a big rabbit’ on the Moon, referring to the Chang’e legend. The astronauts replied that they would keep a close eye out."]],
sp:["The longest-lived lunar rover","Yutu-2, which landed in 2019, operated far longer than its planned three months, surviving more than 50 lunar nights, each about two weeks of freezing darkness."],
g:[["Soft landing","A controlled, gentle landing"],["Far side","The side of the Moon facing away from Earth"],["Relay satellite","A satellite that passes on signals"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"]],
f:["Chang’e 4 made the first far-side landing.","Chang’e 6 returned the first far-side samples.","The first plants to sprout on the Moon grew on Chang’e 4."],
fun:"The cotton seeds aboard Chang’e 4 sprouted in January 2019, making them the first plants ever to grow on the Moon."});

ADDART({id:"lunaprogram",t:"The Soviet Luna Program",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌑",w:"Luna programme",r:["moon","space","sputnik","marsrovers","apollo"],
p:[["Many Moon firsts","The Soviet Luna probes achieved many of the first Moon missions."],["First photos of the far side","Luna 3 photographed the Moon’s hidden side in 1959."],["Robots on the Moon","Lunokhod 1 was the first rover on another world."]],
l:"The Luna program was a series of robotic spacecraft sent to the Moon by the Soviet Union between 1959 and 1976, achieving many historic firsts. Luna 1, launched in January 1959, missed the Moon but became the first spacecraft to escape Earth’s gravity and orbit the Sun. Luna 2 became the first human-made object to reach the Moon when it crashed there on 13 September 1959. In October 1959, Luna 3 took the first photographs of the Moon’s far side, which no human had ever seen. Luna 9 made the first soft landing on the Moon on 3 February 1966 and sent back the first pictures from its surface, proving that the ground could support a spacecraft. Luna 10 became the first spacecraft to orbit the Moon. Luna 16 returned the first robotic lunar sample in 1970, and Luna 17 delivered Lunokhod 1, the first remote-controlled rover on another world. The last mission, Luna 24, returned samples in 1976. Russia revived the name with Luna 25, which crashed on the Moon in August 2023.",
s:[["Luna 3’s photographs","Luna 3 photographed the far side with a film camera, developed the film on board, scanned it and transmitted the images by radio. The blurry pictures revealed that the far side has few dark ‘seas’, unlike the near side. Features were given Soviet names, such as the Moscow Sea."],["Luna 9","Luna 9 used an airbag-like system to bounce to a stop and opened petals to steady itself, then sent panoramic images. British astronomers at Jodrell Bank intercepted the signals and published the first picture before the Soviet Union did."],["Robotic samples","Luna 16, 20 and 24 drilled into the surface and launched small capsules back to Earth with a total of about 300 grams of lunar soil, much less than the 382 kilograms brought by Apollo astronauts, but demonstrating robotic sample return."],["Lunokhod","Lunokhod 1, landed in November 1970, was an eight-wheeled rover controlled from Earth by teams of drivers coping with delays of several seconds. It traveled about 10 kilometers. Lunokhod 2, in 1973, traveled about 39 kilometers, a distance record that stood for decades."]],
k:[["Years","1959–1976"],["First to reach the Moon","Luna 2, 13 September 1959"],["First far-side photos","Luna 3, October 1959"],["First soft landing","Luna 9, 3 February 1966"],["First rover","Lunokhod 1, 1970"]],
d:[["1959","Luna 1, 2 and 3: escape, impact and far-side photos"],["1966","Luna 9 lands softly; Luna 10 orbits the Moon"],["1970","Luna 16 returns samples; Lunokhod 1 lands"],["1973","Lunokhod 2 lands"],["1976","Luna 24, the last mission"],["2023","Russia’s Luna 25 crashes"]],
s2:[["A pennant on the Moon","Luna 2 carried metal pennants with the Soviet coat of arms, designed to scatter on impact. A replica was given to US President Dwight Eisenhower by Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev."],["Lunokhod’s reflector","Lunokhod rovers carried French-made laser reflectors. Scientists still bounce lasers off Lunokhod 2’s reflector, and in 2010 they found Lunokhod 1’s, lost for nearly 40 years."]],
sp:["Luna 15 and Apollo 11","In July 1969, the Soviet Luna 15 was orbiting the Moon at the same time as Apollo 11, attempting to land and collect samples. It crashed into the Moon just hours before Armstrong and Aldrin left the surface."],
g:[["Soft landing","Landing gently without damage"],["Far side","The hemisphere of the Moon facing away from Earth"],["Rover","A vehicle that drives on another world"],["Sample return","Bringing material from space to Earth"]],
f:["Luna 2 was the first object to reach the Moon.","Luna 3 first photographed the far side.","Lunokhod 1 was the first rover on another world."],
fun:"British astronomers at Jodrell Bank decoded Luna 9’s pictures using a newspaper fax machine and published them before the Soviet Union did."});

ADDART({id:"dsn",t:"The Deep Space Network",c:"Space Exploration",i:"📡",w:"NASA Deep Space Network",r:["radioastronomy","voyager","radio","australiacountry","spaincountry"],
p:[["Talking to spacecraft","The Deep Space Network is NASA’s system of giant antennas for communicating with distant probes."],["Three sites","Its antennas are in California, Spain and Australia."],["Always in touch","Together they keep contact as Earth rotates."]],
l:"The Deep Space Network (DSN) is an international network of giant radio antennas run by NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory to communicate with spacecraft exploring the solar system and beyond, as well as some missions around the Moon and Earth. It has three main complexes, spaced roughly 120 degrees apart around the globe: Goldstone in California’s Mojave Desert, near Madrid in Spain, and near Canberra in Australia. As Earth rotates, at least one site can always see any distant spacecraft. Each complex has several dish antennas, including one 70 meters wide, one of the largest steerable antennas in the world, and several 34-meter dishes. The DSN sends commands to spacecraft and receives their data, which can be incredibly faint: the signal from Voyager 1, more than 25 billion kilometers away, arrives with a power of less than a billionth of a billionth of a watt, and takes nearly a day to reach Earth. The network also tracks spacecraft positions precisely and conducts radar studies of asteroids. Demand from new missions has stretched the DSN’s capacity, and NASA is building new antennas.",
s:[["How it works","Huge dishes collect radio waves and focus them onto receivers cooled to very low temperatures to reduce noise. Powerful transmitters, up to 400 kilowatts at Goldstone, send commands. Spacecraft distances are measured by timing signals to within meters."],["History","JPL set up tracking stations for early US probes in 1958, and the network became the Deep Space Network in 1963. Its stations relayed signals from the Apollo missions, including parts of the Apollo 11 broadcast, and have supported nearly every NASA planetary mission."],["Voyager’s whisper","Voyager 1’s 23-watt transmitter is about as powerful as a refrigerator light bulb. By the time its signal reaches Earth, it is so faint that only the large DSN dishes can detect it, often combining several antennas."],["Under pressure","With dozens of missions to the Moon, Mars and beyond competing for antenna time, the DSN is often oversubscribed. NASA is adding new 34-meter antennas and testing laser communication, which can send much more data."]],
k:[["Operated by","NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory"],["Sites","Goldstone (USA), Madrid (Spain), Canberra (Australia)"],["Largest antennas","70 m"],["Founded as DSN","1963"],["Spacing","About 120 degrees apart"]],
d:[["1958","JPL sets up deep space tracking stations"],["1963","Deep Space Network established"],["1966","First 64-meter antenna at Goldstone"],["1969","DSN supports Apollo 11"],["1988","Antennas enlarged to 70 m for Voyager’s Neptune flyby"],["2020","Canberra’s 70-meter dish upgraded"]],
s2:[["Daily schedules","Every week, DSN schedulers juggle requests from dozens of spacecraft, deciding which antennas will talk to which missions. The public can watch live activity on the ‘DSN Now’ website."],["Other networks","ESA, Japan, China, India and Russia operate their own deep space antennas, and agencies often share them in emergencies or for important events."]],
sp:["Talking to Voyager 2","Voyager 2 is so far south in the sky that only the Canberra complex can send it commands, using its 70-meter antenna. When that dish was upgraded in 2020, Voyager 2 could not receive commands for months."],
g:[["Antenna","A device for sending or receiving radio waves"],["Downlink","Data sent from a spacecraft to Earth"],["Uplink","Commands sent from Earth to a spacecraft"],["Oversubscribed","Having more demand than capacity"]],
f:["The DSN has sites in California, Spain and Australia.","Its largest dishes are 70 meters wide.","Voyager 1’s signal takes almost a day to reach Earth."],
fun:"The signal received from Voyager 1 is about 20 billion times weaker than the power needed to run a digital watch."});

ADDART({id:"trappist1",t:"TRAPPIST-1",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🪐",w:"TRAPPIST-1",r:["exoplanet","reddwarfs","habitablezone","jwst","astrobio","superearths"],
p:[["Seven Earth-sized planets","TRAPPIST-1 is a small star with seven rocky planets."],["Close and compact","All seven orbit closer to their star than Mercury does to the Sun."],["Searching for atmospheres","The James Webb Space Telescope is studying them for air."]],
l:"TRAPPIST-1 is a small, cool red dwarf star about 40 light-years away in the constellation Aquarius, famous for having seven known rocky planets, the most Earth-sized planets found around a single star. The star is only a little larger than Jupiter and about 9 percent of the Sun’s mass, glowing a dim red. Belgian astronomers led by Michaël Gillon discovered three planets in 2016 using the TRAPPIST telescope in Chile, after which the system is named; on 22 February 2017, they announced that the system had seven planets, named TRAPPIST-1b to h, detected as they passed in front of their star. All seven orbit extremely close to the star, with years lasting from 1.5 to about 19 days, and they are probably tidally locked, with one side always facing the star. Three or four of them, especially planets e, f and g, lie in the star’s habitable zone, where liquid water might exist on their surfaces. The James Webb Space Telescope found in 2023 that the innermost planets b and c probably lack thick atmospheres, and astronomers continue to study the others, though the star’s strong flares may strip atmospheres away.",
s:[["A compact system","The whole TRAPPIST-1 system would fit inside Mercury’s orbit. From the surface of one planet, the others would appear in the sky larger than the Moon does from Earth. The planets’ orbits are linked in a chain of resonances, suggesting they formed farther out and migrated inward."],["Habitable zone planets","Because the star is so dim, its habitable zone is very close. TRAPPIST-1e is considered one of the most promising: about Earth’s size and receiving a similar amount of energy. Whether any planet has water depends on whether it kept an atmosphere."],["JWST observations","Measurements of heat from TRAPPIST-1b and c in 2023 suggested they are bare rocks or have only thin atmospheres. Studying the outer planets requires many observations as they pass in front of the star, which JWST is carrying out."],["Red dwarf risks","Young red dwarfs emit strong flares and ultraviolet radiation, which may strip planets of atmospheres and water. TRAPPIST-1 is billions of years old, older than the Sun, so its planets have endured this for a long time."]],
k:[["Distance","About 40 light-years"],["Star type","Ultracool red dwarf"],["Planets","Seven, all rocky"],["Habitable zone planets","Three or four (e, f, g, possibly d)"],["Announced","22 February 2017"]],
d:[["1999","TRAPPIST-1 star first catalogued"],["2016","Three planets discovered"],["2017","Seven planets announced"],["2018","Studies estimate planet densities"],["2023","JWST finds TRAPPIST-1b and c lack thick atmospheres"]],
s2:[["Named after a beer?","The TRAPPIST telescope’s name stands for Transiting Planets and Planetesimals Small Telescope, but it was also chosen to honor Belgium’s Trappist beers brewed by monks."],["Travel posters","NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory created imaginary retro travel posters for exoplanets, including one advertising TRAPPIST-1e, to celebrate the discovery."]],
sp:["Planets close together","The TRAPPIST-1 planets pass so close to one another that they tug on each other’s orbits, changing the timing of their transits. Astronomers use these timing changes to measure the planets’ masses."],
g:[["Red dwarf","A small, cool star"],["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Tidally locked","Always showing the same face to the star"],["Resonance","A regular relationship between orbits"]],
f:["TRAPPIST-1 has seven rocky planets.","Its planets orbit closer than Mercury does to the Sun.","TRAPPIST-1 is older than the Sun."],
fun:"A year on TRAPPIST-1b lasts only about 36 hours."});

ADDART({id:"habitablezone",t:"The Habitable Zone",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌍",w:"Habitable zone",r:["exoplanet","astrobio","venus","mars","keplertelescope","superearths","proximab"],
p:[["The ‘Goldilocks zone’","The habitable zone is the region around a star where liquid water could exist on a planet."],["Not too hot, not too cold","Earth lies inside the Sun’s habitable zone."],["Only a starting point","Being in the zone does not guarantee a planet can support life."]],
l:"The habitable zone, often called the ‘Goldilocks zone’, is the range of distances from a star where a rocky planet with a suitable atmosphere could have liquid water on its surface: not so close that water boils away, and not so far that it freezes. Because liquid water is essential for life as we know it, astronomers use the habitable zone to focus the search for potentially habitable exoplanets. The concept was developed in the 1950s, and the term was used by Su-Shu Huang in 1959. The zone’s location depends on the star’s brightness: around hot, bright stars it lies far out, while around dim red dwarfs it is very close. In our solar system, Earth sits comfortably inside the Sun’s habitable zone, while Venus is too hot and Mars, near the outer edge, is now cold and dry. The Kepler space telescope found that many stars have planets in their habitable zones, and scientists estimate there may be billions of potentially habitable rocky planets in the Milky Way. However, a planet’s habitability also depends on its size, atmosphere, magnetic field and history.",
s:[["Why water?","Water dissolves many chemicals and allows complex chemistry, and every living thing on Earth needs it. Astronomers therefore look first for planets that could have liquid water, although life elsewhere might be very different."],["Edges of the zone","The inner edge is set by a runaway greenhouse effect, as happened on Venus; the outer edge by a planet becoming too cold even with a thick greenhouse atmosphere. Estimates for the Sun’s zone range from about 0.95 to 1.7 times Earth’s distance from the Sun."],["Beyond the zone","Moons such as Jupiter’s Europa and Saturn’s Enceladus lie far outside the habitable zone, yet they have liquid water oceans beneath their ice, warmed by tidal heating. This shows that the habitable zone is only one guide to where life might exist."],["Moving zones","As stars age, they grow brighter, pushing the habitable zone outward. In about a billion years, the Sun will be bright enough to make Earth too hot for liquid oceans."]],
k:[["Nickname","Goldilocks zone"],["Defined by","Possibility of surface liquid water"],["Term used by","Su-Shu Huang, 1959"],["In our solar system","Earth (Mars near the edge)"],["Potentially habitable planets","Possibly billions in the Milky Way"]],
d:[["1953","Hubertus Strughold describes an ‘ecosphere’"],["1959","Su-Shu Huang writes about the habitable zone"],["1993","James Kasting refines habitable zone calculations"],["2011","Kepler-22b found in a Sun-like star’s habitable zone"],["2016","Proxima b found in its star’s habitable zone"]],
s2:[["Galactic habitable zone","Some scientists suggest there is also a galactic habitable zone, a region of a galaxy with enough heavy elements to form rocky planets but far from dangerous supernovae and crowded star regions. The Sun lies in such a region."],["Earth’s luck","Earth has remained habitable for billions of years thanks to its distance from the Sun, its protective magnetic field, plate tectonics that recycle carbon and a large moon that stabilizes its tilt."]],
sp:["Goldilocks","The nickname comes from the fairy tale ‘Goldilocks and the Three Bears’, in which a girl chooses porridge that is ‘just right’, neither too hot nor too cold."],
g:[["Habitable zone","Where a planet could have liquid surface water"],["Exoplanet","A planet around another star"],["Runaway greenhouse effect","Uncontrolled heating from trapped heat"],["Tidal heating","Heat produced by gravitational squeezing"]],
f:["Earth lies in the Sun’s habitable zone.","Red dwarfs have habitable zones very close to them.","Being in the zone does not guarantee life."],
fun:"If Earth were about 5 percent closer to the Sun, some scientists think it could have suffered a runaway greenhouse effect like Venus."});

ADDART({id:"reddwarfs",t:"Red Dwarfs",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔴",w:"Red dwarf",r:["stars","alphacentauri","trappist1","habitablezone","browndwarfs","proximab"],
p:[["The most common stars","Red dwarfs make up about three-quarters of the stars in the Milky Way."],["Small and dim","None can be seen with the naked eye from Earth."],["Almost eternal","They can shine for trillions of years."]],
l:"Red dwarfs are small, cool, dim stars, with between about 8 and 60 percent of the Sun’s mass and surface temperatures below about 3,900°C. They are by far the most common type of star, making up about 75 percent of the stars in the Milky Way, yet none is bright enough to be seen with the naked eye from Earth. The closest star to the Sun, Proxima Centauri, about 4.2 light-years away, is a red dwarf, as are Barnard’s Star and TRAPPIST-1. Red dwarfs burn their hydrogen fuel very slowly, and because the material in them is constantly mixed, they can use almost all of it; the smallest may shine for trillions of years, far longer than the current age of the universe, about 13.8 billion years, so no red dwarf has yet died of old age. Many red dwarfs have planets, including rocky planets in their habitable zones, making them important targets in the search for life. However, red dwarfs often produce powerful flares that could strip planets’ atmospheres, and planets close enough to be warm are likely tidally locked.",
s:[["How red dwarfs shine","Like the Sun, red dwarfs fuse hydrogen into helium in their cores, but at much lower temperatures and rates. Energy is carried by convection throughout most of the star, mixing fresh hydrogen into the core."],["Flares","Many red dwarfs are flare stars, suddenly brightening as magnetic energy is released. Proxima Centauri has produced flares that briefly made it many times brighter. Such flares and ultraviolet radiation could make nearby planets hard places for life."],["Planets around red dwarfs","Because red dwarfs are small, planets passing in front of them block more light, making them easier to detect. Proxima Centauri b, discovered in 2016, orbits in Proxima’s habitable zone, and TRAPPIST-1 has seven rocky planets."],["The future","When red dwarfs finally exhaust their fuel, trillions of years from now, they are expected to become blue dwarfs and then white dwarfs, without swelling into red giants."]],
k:[["Mass","About 8–60% of the Sun’s"],["Share of stars","About 75% of the Milky Way"],["Nearest example","Proxima Centauri"],["Lifespan","Up to trillions of years"],["Visible to the naked eye","None"]],
d:[["1915","Proxima Centauri discovered"],["1916","Barnard’s Star’s fast motion measured"],["2016","Proxima Centauri b discovered"],["2017","TRAPPIST-1’s seven planets announced"],["2024","Planets confirmed around Barnard’s Star"]],
s2:[["Barnard’s Star","Barnard’s Star, about 6 light-years away, moves faster across the sky than any other star. In 2024 and 2025, astronomers confirmed several small planets orbiting it, after decades of false claims."],["A quiet life","Older red dwarfs tend to become calmer, with fewer flares, so planets around older red dwarfs might be more hospitable than those around young ones."]],
sp:["Too dim to see","Even Proxima Centauri, the nearest star, is about 100 times too faint to see without a telescope. Its bright neighbors, Alpha Centauri A and B, are Sun-like stars."],
g:[["Red dwarf","A small, cool, long-lived star"],["Flare star","A star that brightens suddenly with flares"],["Convection","Heat transfer by moving material"],["White dwarf","The dense core left after a star dies"]],
f:["Red dwarfs are about 75% of the stars in the Milky Way.","The nearest star, Proxima Centauri, is a red dwarf.","No red dwarf has yet died of old age."],
fun:"The universe is not yet old enough for any red dwarf to have run out of fuel."});

ADDART({id:"starformation",t:"How Stars Are Born",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"⭐",w:"Star formation",r:["nebula","stars","jwst","solarform","grav","starlifecycle","galaxymergers"],
p:[["Born in clouds","Stars form inside cold, dense clouds of gas and dust."],["Gravity pulls together","Clumps collapse and heat up until nuclear fusion begins."],["Planets too","Disks around young stars give birth to planets."]],
l:"Stars form inside giant clouds of cold gas and dust called molecular clouds, or stellar nurseries, made mostly of hydrogen. When part of a cloud becomes dense enough, perhaps triggered by a nearby supernova or collision with another cloud, gravity causes it to collapse. As the clump shrinks, it heats up and spins faster, forming a hot core called a protostar surrounded by a flattened, rotating disk of gas and dust. Over hundreds of thousands to millions of years, the protostar grows by pulling in material, often blasting out jets from its poles. When the temperature in its core reaches about 10 million degrees Celsius, hydrogen fusion begins, and a true star is born. The leftover disk around the young star can form planets, asteroids and comets; this is how the Sun and solar system formed about 4.6 billion years ago. Famous star-forming regions include the Orion Nebula, about 1,350 light-years away, and the ‘Pillars of Creation’ in the Eagle Nebula. Infrared telescopes such as the James Webb Space Telescope can see through dust into stellar nurseries. The Milky Way forms roughly one or two Sun-masses of new stars a year.",
s:[["Molecular clouds","Molecular clouds can be hundreds of light-years across and contain enough gas to make thousands or millions of stars, at temperatures only about 10 to 20 degrees above absolute zero. Their dust blocks visible light, which is why they appear as dark patches against the Milky Way."],["Protoplanetary disks","In 2014, the ALMA radio telescope array in Chile photographed HL Tauri, a young star surrounded by a disk with clear gaps, probably carved by forming planets, giving astronomers a direct view of planet formation."],["Star clusters","Stars usually form in groups. The Pleiades, visible to the naked eye, is a young cluster about 100 million years old. Over time, clusters drift apart; the Sun’s siblings have long since spread across the galaxy."],["Pillars of Creation","The Hubble Space Telescope’s 1995 image of towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, where new stars are forming, became one of the most famous astronomical images. JWST photographed them again in 2022 in infrared, revealing newborn stars inside."]],
k:[["Birthplace","Molecular clouds"],["Young star","Protostar"],["Fusion begins at","About 10 million °C"],["Famous nursery","Orion Nebula"],["Milky Way star formation","About 1–2 Sun-masses a year"]],
d:[["1755","Immanuel Kant proposes the solar system formed from a spinning cloud"],["1796","Pierre-Simon Laplace develops the nebular hypothesis"],["1995","Hubble’s ‘Pillars of Creation’ image"],["2014","ALMA images the disk around HL Tauri"],["2022","JWST images the Pillars of Creation"]],
s2:[["Massive stars","Massive stars form more quickly and burn brightly for only a few million years. Their intense radiation and winds can blow away the surrounding cloud, stopping or triggering the formation of other stars nearby."],["The Orion Nebula","The Orion Nebula is visible to the naked eye as a fuzzy patch in Orion’s Sword. Inside it, the four bright Trapezium stars light up the gas, and Hubble found more than 150 protoplanetary disks there."]],
sp:["Herbig–Haro objects","Jets shot out from young stars smash into surrounding gas, creating glowing knots called Herbig–Haro objects, named after astronomers George Herbig and Guillermo Haro."],
g:[["Molecular cloud","A cold, dense cloud where stars form"],["Protostar","A young star still gathering mass"],["Protoplanetary disk","A disk of gas and dust around a young star"],["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"]],
f:["Stars form in cold clouds of gas and dust.","Fusion begins at about 10 million degrees.","The Orion Nebula is a stellar nursery."],
fun:"The Sun was probably born in a cluster with thousands of sibling stars, which have since scattered across the Milky Way."});

ADDART({id:"fateuniverse",t:"The Fate of the Universe",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"♾️",w:"Ultimate fate of the universe",r:["bigbang","darkmatter","hubble","bh","cmb"],
p:[["How will it end?","Scientists study whether the universe will expand forever, tear apart or collapse."],["Dark energy decides","The mysterious dark energy driving expansion will shape the future."],["Unimaginably far off","Any end would come many billions of years from now."]],
l:"The ultimate fate of the universe is a topic of cosmology, the study of the universe as a whole. The universe has been expanding since the Big Bang about 13.8 billion years ago. In 1998, two teams of astronomers studying distant supernovae discovered that the expansion is accelerating, driven by a mysterious ‘dark energy’ that makes up about 68 percent of the universe’s content; they won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics. If dark energy stays constant, as most measurements have suggested, the universe will expand forever and gradually cool in a ‘Big Freeze’ or ‘heat death’: galaxies will drift out of sight, stars will burn out, and after unimaginably long times even black holes will evaporate, leaving a cold, dark, nearly empty universe. If dark energy grows stronger, it could tear apart galaxies, stars and even atoms in a ‘Big Rip’. If it weakens and reverses, gravity could pull everything back together in a ‘Big Crunch’. In 2024 and 2025, results from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument (DESI) hinted that dark energy may be weakening over time, a possibility scientists are testing with new observations.",
s:[["The Big Freeze","In about 100 trillion years, star formation will end as gas runs out. Over far longer times, stars will become white dwarfs, neutron stars and black holes, and black holes will slowly evaporate through Hawking radiation over about 10¹⁰⁰ years, leaving only scattered particles and radiation."],["The Big Rip","If dark energy becomes stronger over time, the expansion could eventually overcome gravity holding galaxies together, then solar systems, planets and finally atoms. Current measurements make this unlikely but not impossible."],["The Big Crunch","Before dark energy was discovered, many cosmologists thought gravity might eventually halt the expansion and reverse it, collapsing the universe into a hot, dense state, possibly leading to a new Big Bang in a ‘Big Bounce’. Weakening dark energy could revive this possibility."],["Nearer futures","The Sun will swell into a red giant in about 5 billion years. A 2025 study found that the Milky Way and the Andromeda Galaxy have about a 50 percent chance of merging within 10 billion years, less certain than previously thought."]],
k:[["Universe’s age","About 13.8 billion years"],["Accelerating expansion discovered","1998"],["Dark energy share","About 68%"],["Main scenarios","Big Freeze, Big Rip, Big Crunch"],["New hint","DESI suggests dark energy may be weakening"]],
d:[["1917","Einstein adds the cosmological constant"],["1929","Hubble finds the universe is expanding"],["1998","Accelerating expansion discovered"],["2011","Nobel Prize for discovering accelerating expansion"],["2024","DESI hints dark energy may be changing"]],
s2:[["Einstein’s ‘biggest blunder’","Einstein added a ‘cosmological constant’ to his equations in 1917 to keep the universe static. After Hubble found expansion, he reportedly called it his biggest blunder. Today, the cosmological constant is the simplest explanation for dark energy."],["Future astronomers","Because of accelerating expansion, in about 100 billion years, all galaxies beyond our local group will have receded beyond view. Astronomers then might see only one giant merged galaxy and have no evidence of the Big Bang."]],
sp:["Vacuum decay","A more speculative idea is ‘vacuum decay’: if the universe is not in its most stable state, a bubble of a more stable state could form and expand at the speed of light, changing the laws of physics. Physicists consider it very unlikely in any foreseeable time."],
g:[["Cosmology","The study of the universe as a whole"],["Dark energy","A mysterious force driving accelerating expansion"],["Heat death","A state of no usable energy"],["Hawking radiation","Radiation slowly emitted by black holes"]],
f:["The universe’s expansion is accelerating.","Dark energy makes up about 68% of the universe.","Black holes may evaporate after about 10¹⁰⁰ years."],
fun:"In the far future, astronomers in our galaxy may see no other galaxies at all, because all others will have been carried out of view by expansion."});

ADDART({id:"groundtelescopes",t:"Giant Ground Telescopes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🔭",w:"Extremely large telescope",r:["scope","jwst","lightpollution","hawaii","chilecountry"],
p:[["Bigger mirrors, deeper views","Giant telescopes collect more light to see faint, distant objects."],["High and dry","They are built on mountaintops in Chile, Hawaii and the Canary Islands."],["The next giants","The Extremely Large Telescope, with a 39-meter mirror, is being built in Chile."]],
l:"Ground-based telescopes have grown enormously since Galileo’s small telescope of 1609. A telescope’s light-gathering power depends on the area of its main mirror, so larger mirrors reveal fainter and more distant objects in greater detail. The 5-meter Hale Telescope at Palomar Observatory in California was the world’s largest from 1948 to 1975. Modern giants include the twin 10-meter Keck telescopes on Mauna Kea in Hawaii, whose mirrors are made of 36 hexagonal segments; the 10.4-meter Gran Telescopio Canarias in Spain’s Canary Islands; Japan’s Subaru; and the European Southern Observatory’s Very Large Telescope in Chile, four 8.2-meter telescopes that can work together. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which released its first images in June 2025 with the largest digital camera ever built, is surveying the whole southern sky every few nights. The next generation includes the Extremely Large Telescope (ELT), with a 39-meter mirror, under construction in Chile’s Atacama Desert and expected to see first light around the end of the 2020s, and the 25-meter Giant Magellan Telescope. The Thirty Meter Telescope planned for Mauna Kea has been delayed by protests and funding uncertainty.",
s:[["Where telescopes are built","Observatories are built on high, dry mountains with clear, steady air, far from city lights, such as Chile’s Atacama Desert, Mauna Kea in Hawaii and La Palma in the Canary Islands. Chile hosts a large share of the world’s major telescopes."],["Adaptive optics","Earth’s turbulent atmosphere blurs starlight, making stars twinkle. Adaptive optics systems shine lasers into the sky to create artificial guide stars and use deformable mirrors that change shape hundreds of times a second to cancel the blurring, letting ground telescopes rival space telescopes in sharpness."],["The ELT","The ELT’s main mirror will be made of 798 hexagonal segments. It will collect more light than all existing large optical telescopes combined and could photograph some exoplanets directly and study their atmospheres for signs of life."],["Mauna Kea controversy","Mauna Kea is sacred to many Native Hawaiians. Protests against building the Thirty Meter Telescope, especially a long blockade in 2019, led to a new state authority sharing management of the mountain with Native Hawaiian representatives. The US National Science Foundation chose in 2025 to advance the Giant Magellan Telescope."]],
k:[["Keck telescopes","Two 10-meter telescopes, Hawaii"],["Largest single optical telescope","Gran Telescopio Canarias, 10.4 m"],["Very Large Telescope","Four 8.2-meter telescopes, Chile"],["Rubin Observatory first images","June 2025"],["Coming giant","Extremely Large Telescope, 39 m"]],
d:[["1609","Galileo turns a telescope to the sky"],["1948","5-meter Hale Telescope completed"],["1993","First Keck telescope begins observations"],["1998","Very Large Telescope sees first light"],["2009","Gran Telescopio Canarias opens"],["2025","Vera C. Rubin Observatory releases first images"]],
s2:[["The Rubin camera","The Rubin Observatory’s camera has 3,200 megapixels; displaying one of its images at full resolution would need hundreds of high-definition TV screens. It is expected to discover millions of asteroids and billions of galaxies."],["Arecibo","The giant Arecibo radio telescope in Puerto Rico, 305 meters across, collapsed in December 2020 after cables failed, ending 57 years of observations."]],
sp:["Why mirrors, not lenses","Large telescopes use mirrors rather than lenses because big lenses sag under their own weight and bend different colors differently. Mirrors can be supported from behind and made in segments."],
g:[["Primary mirror","The main light-collecting mirror"],["Adaptive optics","Technology that corrects atmospheric blurring"],["Segmented mirror","A mirror made of many smaller pieces"],["First light","A telescope’s first observation"]],
f:["The ELT’s mirror will be 39 meters wide.","Adaptive optics uses lasers to sharpen images.","The Rubin Observatory has the largest digital camera ever built."],
fun:"The ELT will be able to collect about 100 million times more light than the human eye."});

ADDART({id:"orbits",t:"Orbits and Orbital Mechanics",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🌀",w:"Orbit",r:["grav","keplerlaws","sat","lagrange","newton"],
p:[["Always falling","An orbit is a path in which an object constantly falls around a planet or star."],["Kepler and Newton","Kepler described orbits; Newton explained them with gravity."],["Kinds of orbits","Satellites use low, medium, geostationary and polar orbits."]],
l:"An orbit is the curved path of an object, such as a planet, moon or satellite, around another object under the influence of gravity. An orbiting object is constantly falling toward the body it orbits but moving sideways so fast that it keeps missing it. In the early 17th century, Johannes Kepler showed that planets move in ellipses with the Sun at one focus, and in 1687 Isaac Newton explained orbits through his laws of motion and universal gravitation, imagining a cannonball fired so fast from a mountain that it would circle Earth. To stay in low Earth orbit, a spacecraft must travel about 7.8 kilometers per second; the International Space Station, about 400 kilometers up, circles Earth every 90 minutes. Higher orbits are slower. At about 35,786 kilometers, a geostationary satellite circles once a day, appearing fixed in the sky, an idea popularized by Arthur C. Clarke in 1945. To escape Earth entirely requires about 11.2 kilometers per second. Spacecraft travel between planets using carefully calculated transfer orbits and gravity assists, which use a planet’s gravity to change speed and direction.",
s:[["Types of Earth orbit","Low Earth orbit, up to about 2,000 kilometers, hosts the ISS, imaging satellites and megaconstellations. Medium Earth orbit hosts navigation satellites such as GPS at about 20,200 kilometers. Geostationary orbit is used for weather and communications satellites. Polar and Sun-synchronous orbits pass over the poles, letting satellites image the whole Earth."],["Transfer orbits","In 1925, German engineer Walter Hohmann calculated the most fuel-efficient way to move between two circular orbits, now called a Hohmann transfer. Missions to Mars use such paths, launching when the planets are correctly aligned, about every 26 months."],["Gravity assists","A spacecraft passing close to a planet can gain or lose speed relative to the Sun by ‘stealing’ a tiny amount of the planet’s orbital energy. Mariner 10 used Venus in 1974 to reach Mercury, and Voyager 2 used Jupiter, Saturn and Uranus to reach Neptune."],["Counterintuitive maneuvers","In orbit, to catch up with something ahead, a spacecraft must first slow down, dropping to a lower, faster orbit. Speeding up raises the orbit and makes the spacecraft fall behind. Astronauts must learn these rules for docking."]],
k:[["Low Earth orbit speed","About 7.8 km/s"],["ISS orbit","About 400 km, every 90 minutes"],["Geostationary altitude","About 35,786 km"],["Escape velocity","About 11.2 km/s"],["Explained by","Newton’s laws (1687)"]],
d:[["1609","Kepler publishes his first two laws of planetary motion"],["1687","Newton publishes the Principia"],["1925","Hohmann describes transfer orbits"],["1945","Arthur C. Clarke proposes geostationary satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 becomes the first artificial satellite"],["1974","Mariner 10 makes the first gravity assist"]],
s2:[["Newton’s cannonball","In his thought experiment, Newton imagined a cannon on a very high mountain. Fired slowly, the ball falls nearby; faster, it lands farther away; fast enough, it falls around the whole Earth and returns to the mountain, orbiting."],["The Clarke belt","Geostationary orbit is sometimes called the Clarke orbit or Clarke belt, after science fiction writer Arthur C. Clarke, who described in 1945 how three satellites there could provide worldwide communications."]],
sp:["Why astronauts float","Astronauts on the ISS are not beyond Earth’s gravity, which is still about 90 percent as strong there. They float because they and their spacecraft are falling together around Earth."],
g:[["Orbit","A curved path around a body due to gravity"],["Ellipse","An oval shape"],["Escape velocity","The speed needed to leave a body’s gravity"],["Geostationary","Orbiting at the same rate Earth rotates"]],
f:["Orbiting objects are constantly falling.","The ISS orbits Earth every 90 minutes.","To catch up in orbit, you slow down."],
fun:"Gravity on the International Space Station is still about 90 percent as strong as on Earth’s surface; astronauts float because they are in free fall."});

ADDART({id:"rocketengines",t:"Rocket Engines",c:"Space Exploration",i:"🔥",w:"Rocket engine",r:["rocket","saturnv","starship","ionengines","jetengine"],
p:[["Pushing by throwing","Rocket engines push forward by blasting hot gas backward."],["Carrying their own oxygen","Unlike jet engines, rockets work in the vacuum of space."],["Solid, liquid and more","Engines burn solid propellant, liquid fuels or use electricity."]],
l:"A rocket engine produces thrust by expelling hot gas or other material at high speed from a nozzle, following Isaac Newton’s third law of motion: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. Unlike jet engines, which take oxygen from the air, rockets carry both fuel and an oxidizer, so they can work in the vacuum of space. Gunpowder rockets were invented in China around the 13th century. Russian scientist Konstantin Tsiolkovsky worked out the basic rocket equation in 1903, and American physicist Robert Goddard launched the first liquid-fueled rocket on 16 March 1926 in Massachusetts. Germany’s V-2 missile of World War II was the first large liquid-fueled rocket and the forerunner of space rockets. Solid rocket motors are simple and powerful but cannot be throttled or shut down easily. Liquid engines, which pump fuels such as kerosene, liquid hydrogen or methane with liquid oxygen into a combustion chamber, can be controlled and restarted. Famous engines include the Saturn V’s F-1, the Space Shuttle’s RS-25 and SpaceX’s Raptor. Electric propulsion such as ion engines gives tiny thrust with very high efficiency.",
s:[["How a liquid engine works","Turbopumps spinning at tens of thousands of revolutions per minute force fuel and oxidizer into the combustion chamber, where they burn at temperatures above 3,000°C. The hot gas accelerates through a bell-shaped nozzle to speeds of 3 to 4.5 kilometers per second. Some fuel often flows around the nozzle first to keep it from melting."],["Propellants","Kerosene with liquid oxygen gives high thrust, used by the F-1 and Falcon 9’s Merlin engines. Liquid hydrogen gives the highest efficiency but is bulky and must be kept at −253°C. Methane, used by SpaceX’s Raptor and Blue Origin’s BE-4, burns cleanly and could be made on Mars."],["Engine cycles","Engineers design different ways to drive turbopumps. Staged combustion engines, pioneered in the Soviet Union, burn some propellant to power the pumps and then feed the exhaust into the main chamber, achieving high efficiency. SpaceX’s Raptor uses a ‘full-flow staged combustion’ cycle, the first to fly."],["New manufacturing","Many modern engines include 3D-printed parts, reducing costs and allowing complex shapes. Rocket Lab’s Rutherford engine uses battery-powered electric pumps."]],
k:[["Principle","Newton’s third law"],["First liquid-fueled rocket","Robert Goddard, 16 March 1926"],["Rocket equation","Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, 1903"],["Main types","Solid, liquid, hybrid and electric"],["Famous engines","F-1, RS-25, Merlin, Raptor"]],
d:[["c. 1200s","Gunpowder rockets used in China"],["1903","Tsiolkovsky publishes the rocket equation"],["1926","Goddard launches the first liquid-fueled rocket"],["1944","A V-2 test rocket becomes the first object to reach space"],["1981","Space Shuttle’s RS-25 engines fly"],["2019","SpaceX’s Raptor engine first flies"]],
s2:[["Goddard and the New York Times","In 1920, The New York Times mocked Robert Goddard for suggesting rockets could work in space, claiming they needed air to push against. On 17 July 1969, as Apollo 11 flew to the Moon, the newspaper printed a correction."],["Reusing engines","The Space Shuttle’s RS-25 engines were reused many times, and the same engines now power NASA’s Space Launch System, though they are discarded after each flight."]],
sp:["The rocket equation","Tsiolkovsky’s rocket equation shows that a rocket’s change in speed depends on its exhaust speed and how much of its mass is propellant. Because of it, most of a rocket’s launch mass must be fuel, which is why rockets have stages."],
g:[["Thrust","The force that pushes a rocket"],["Oxidizer","A chemical that supplies oxygen for burning"],["Turbopump","A pump driven by a turbine that feeds propellant"],["Nozzle","The bell-shaped exit that accelerates exhaust"]],
f:["Rockets carry their own oxygen.","Goddard launched the first liquid-fueled rocket in 1926.","Most of a rocket’s mass is propellant."],
fun:"Robert Goddard’s first liquid-fueled rocket flew for just 2.5 seconds and reached a height of about 12.5 meters, landing in a cabbage field."});
